## 3. The Four Contract Scenarios

BrandBridge supports **four types of contracts** across **three user roles** (Creator, Brand, Agency). Any registered user can initiate contracts:

### Scenario 1: Creator → Brand

Creator found the brand (or was approached via DM), negotiated terms, and initiates the contract.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Creator
    participant BB as BrandBridge
    participant B as Brand

    Note over C,B: They found each other via Instagram/DM
    C->>BB: Creates contract, fills creator fields
    BB->>B: Sends contract link via email
    B->>BB: Fills brand fields + email OTP + signs
    BB->>C: Notifies creator (email)
    C->>BB: Reviews + signs
    BB->>C: Sends signed PDF via email
    BB->>B: Sends signed PDF via email
```

**Liability**: Direct between creator and brand.

---

### Scenario 2: Brand → Creator

Brand found the creator and wants to formalize the deal. Brand initiates the contract.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant B as Brand
    participant BB as BrandBridge
    participant C as Creator

    Note over B,C: Brand found creator on Instagram / via referral
    B->>BB: Creates contract, fills brand fields + deliverables + usage rights
    BB->>C: Sends contract link via email
    C->>BB: Fills creator fields + email OTP + signs
    BB->>B: Notifies brand (email)
    B->>BB: Reviews + signs
    BB->>B: Sends signed PDF via email
    BB->>C: Sends signed PDF via email
```

**Liability**: Direct between brand and creator.

---

### Scenario 3: Agency → Brand + Agency → Creator (Middleman — Two Contracts)

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant B as Brand
    participant BB as BrandBridge
    participant A as Agency
    participant C as Creator

    Note over B,A: Brand approaches agency, they negotiate off-platform

    rect rgb(40, 40, 80)
        Note over A,B: CONTRACT 1: Brand ↔ Agency
        A->>BB: Creates Brand-Agency contract
        BB->>B: Sends contract link via email
        B->>BB: Fills brand fields + email OTP + signs
        BB->>A: Notifies agency (email)
        A->>BB: Reviews + signs
        BB->>A: Sends signed PDF (email)
        BB->>B: Sends signed PDF (email)
    end

    Note over A,C: Agency approaches creator, they negotiate off-platform

    rect rgb(40, 80, 40)
        Note over A,C: CONTRACT 2: Agency ↔ Creator
        A->>BB: Creates Agency-Creator contract (optionally linked to Contract 1)
        BB->>C: Sends contract link via email
        C->>BB: Fills creator fields + email OTP + signs
        BB->>A: Notifies agency (email)
        A->>BB: Reviews + signs
        BB->>A: Sends signed PDF (email)
        BB->>C: Sends signed PDF (email)
    end
```

### Liability Firewall — The Key Design

```mermaid
graph LR
    subgraph "Content Disputes"
        B["Brand"] <-->|"Contract 1<br/>Brand resolves with Agency"| A1["Agency"]
    end
    
    subgraph "Payment Disputes"
        A2["Agency"] <-->|"Contract 2<br/>Agency resolves with Creator"| C["Creator"]
    end
    
    B -.->|"NO direct relationship<br/>Brand has nothing to do<br/>with creator payment"| C
```

| Dispute Type | Who Resolves | Based On | Who's NOT Involved |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Content not delivered / quality issue** | Brand + Agency | Contract 1 (Brand↔Agency) | Creator has nothing to do with it |
| **Payment not received / delayed** | Agency + Creator | Contract 2 (Agency↔Creator) | Brand has nothing to do with it |
| **Usage rights violation** | Depends on which contract the violation relates to | Contract 1 or 2 | — |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> BrandBridge does NOT enforce this liability separation — it's a natural consequence of having two separate contracts between two separate pairs of parties. Each contract is a standalone legal agreement. The platform just makes it easy to create, sign, and store both.

